      "content='Sure! Below is a curated list of sci‑fi movies spanning the classics, modern blockbusters, hidden gems, and a few standout TV‑movie hybrids. I’ve grouped them by era/style and added a one‑sentence hook (plus the year) so you can quickly spot what sounds appealing.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🎬 Classic & Influential (pre‑2000)\\n\\n| Title | Year | Why it’s worth a watch |\\n|-------|------|------------------------|\\n| **2001: A Space Odyssey** | 1968 | Stanley Kubrick’s visual masterpiece that redefined space‑travel storytelling. |\\n| **Blade\\u202fRunner** | 1982 | A noir‑styled, rain‑slick future that asks “What does it mean to be human?” |\\n| **The Thing** | 1982 | Body‑horror paranoia in an Antarctic outpost—great practical effects. |\\n| **Aliens** | 1986 | Action‑packed sequel that balances terror with a strong female lead (Ripley). |\\n| **The Terminator** | 1984 | Time‑travel thriller with a relentless cyborg and iconic one‑liners. |\\n| **Back to the Future** | 1985 | Light‑hearted time‑travel adventure that’s still a cultural touchstone. |\\n| **The Fly** | 1986 | David Cronenberg’s grotesque metamorphosis tale, both horror and tragedy. |\\n| **Star Wars: Episode\\u202fIV – A New Hope** | 1977 | The space‑opera that launched a franchise and a generation of fans. |\\n| **The Matrix** | 1999 | Ground‑breaking visual effects and a mind‑bending reality‑vs‑simulation premise. |\\n| **Gattaca** | 1997 | A sleek, near‑future look at genetic engineering and destiny. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🚀 Modern Blockbusters (2000‑2015)\\n\\n| Title | Year | Hook |\\n|-------|------|------|\\n| **Minority Report** | 2002 | A near‑future police unit that can arrest people before they commit crimes. |\\n| **Moon** | 2009 | A solitary lunar worker discovers a disturbing secret about his mission. |\\n| **Inception** | 2010 | Heist movie set inside layered dreams—visuals and puzzles galore. |\\n| **District 9** | 2009 | An alien‑refugee camp in Johannesburg becomes a gritty social allegory. |\\n| **Edge of Tomorrow** | 2014 | Time‑loop combat against alien invaders; Tom Cruise’s best‑selling action. |\\n| **Interstellar** | 2014 | Epic space‑travel quest to save humanity, with stunning astrophysics. |\\n| **Ex Machina** | 2014 | A programmer tests a humanoid AI—psychological tension meets sleek design. |\\n| **The Martian** | 2015 | Survival on Mars with humor, science, and a lot of potatoes. |\\n| **Guardians of the Galaxy** | 2014 | Cosmic misfits, retro soundtrack, and a surprisingly heartfelt story. |\\n| **Snowpiercer** (the film) | 2013 | Post‑apocalyptic train society explores class struggle. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🌌 Recent & Cutting‑Edge (2016‑2024)\\n\\n| Title | Year | Hook |\\n|-------|------|------|\\n| **Arrival** | 2016 | Linguist deciphers alien language, reshaping humanity’s perception of time. |\\n| **Blade Runner 2049** | 2017 | Visually breathtaking sequel that expands the original’s philosophical core. |\\n| **Annihilation** | 2018 | A mysterious “shimmer” zone mutates biology—psychological sci‑fi horror. |\\n| **Dune** (Denis Villeneuve) | 2021 | Epic adaptation of Frank Herbert’s desert‑planet saga, with spectacular world‑building. |\\n| **Tenet** | 2020 | Time‑inversion espionage that bends cause and effect. |\\n| **The Midnight Sky** | 2020 | Post‑apocalyptic Earth meets a stranded space crew; visually poetic. |\\n| **Ad Astra** | 2019 | Introspective father‑son odyssey across the solar system. |\\n| **The Wandering Earth** | 2019 (China) | Humanity moves Earth away from an expanding Sun—massive disaster spectacle. |\\n| **Everything Everywhere All at Once** | 2022 | Multiversal chaos meets heartfelt family drama; genre‑bending brilliance. |\\n| **Avatar: The Way of Water** | 2022 | James Cameron’s deep‑sea alien world pushes performance‑capture tech. |\\n| **The Matrix Resurrections** | 2021 | Returns to the simulation with meta commentary on nostalgia. |\\n| **After Yang** | 2021 | Quiet, meditative look at a family dealing with a malfunctioning android child. |\\n| **The Midnight Sky** | 2020 | Post‑apocalyptic Earth meets a stranded space crew; visually poetic. |\\n| **The Forever Purge** | 2021 | (If you like sci‑fi horror) A dystopian future where lawlessness reigns for a night. |\\n| **Spiderhead** | 2022 | A near‑future prison where inmates test mood‑altering drugs. |\\n| **The Adam Project** | 2022 | Time‑travel buddy‑comedy with a teenage hero and his adult self. |\\n| **The Creator** | 2023 | AI‑driven war on Earth, with striking visuals and philosophical undertones. |\\n| **M3GAN** | 2022 | A killer doll/AI that’s both creepy and oddly sympathetic. |\\n| **The Peripheral** (TV, 2022‑2023) | — | Based on William Gibson’s novel; explores near‑future tech & alternate timelines. |\\n| **The Peripheral** (TV, 2022‑2023) | — | Based on William Gibson’s novel; explores near‑future tech & alternate timelines. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 🌠 Hidden Gems & Indie Picks\\n\\n| Title | Year | Hook |\\n|-------|------|------|\\n| **Coherence** | 2013 | A dinner party’s reality unravels after a comet passes—low‑budget, high‑concept. |\\n| **Primer** | 2004 | Two engineers accidentally build a time machine; the most realistic (and confusing) time‑travel film. |\\n| **Timecrimes (Los Cronocrímenes)** | 2007 | Spanish thriller where a man loops into his own past in a tight, claustrophobic plot. |\\n| **Upgrade** | 2018 | Cyber‑enhanced revenge story that mixes body‑horror with slick action. |\\n| **Prospect** | 2018 | A father‑daughter duo hunts for gems on an alien moon; gritty, atmospheric. |\\n| **The Endless** | 2017 | Two brothers return to a cult they escaped; reality‑bending horror. |\\n| **I Am Mother** | 2019 | Post‑apocalypse AI raises a human child—twisty moral dilemmas. |\\n| **Aniara** | 2018 | Swedish adaptation of a poem about a spaceship lost in space; meditative and bleak. |\\n| **The Vast of Night** | 2019 | 1950s small‑town radio crew hears a mysterious signal—retro, atmospheric, and clever. |\\n| **Stowaway** | 2021 | A Mars mission faces a life‑or‑death dilemma when an accidental stowaway appears. |\\n| **The Last Days of Mars** | 2013 | Astronauts on a dying Mars base encounter a deadly pathogen. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 📺 Sci‑Fi TV Movies & Mini‑Series (Great for a Single‑Sitting)\\n\\n| Title | Year | Format | Hook |\\n|-------|------|--------|------|\\n| **Black Mirror: San Junipero** | 2016 | TV episode (but feels like a short film) | A nostalgic love story set in a simulated afterlife. |\\n| **Star Trek: The Motion Picture** | 1979 | Feature film (original series) | The first big‑screen trek—grand, philosophical, and visually iconic. |\\n| **The Man from Earth** | 2007 | Feature film (dialogue‑driven) | A professor reveals he’s been alive for 14,000 years. |\\n| **Solaris (1972 & 2002)** | — | Two versions (Andrei Tarkovsky & Steven Soderbergh) | A haunting exploration of memory and grief on a sentient planet. |\\n| **The Orville: “All the World’s a Stage” (Season\\u202f2, Episode\\u202f3)** | 2018 | TV episode | A meta‑commentary on live‑action role‑playing in a sci‑fi setting. |\\n| **Love, Death & Robots** (selected episodes) | 2019‑2023 | Anthology series | Short, high‑concept animated sci‑fi ranging from horror to comedy. |\\n| **The Expanse** (Season\\u202f1‑3) | 2015‑2018 | Series (but each episode feels like a mini‑movie) | A gritty, politically charged space opera with excellent world‑building. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 📚 How to Choose\\n\\n1. **Mood:**  \\n   - *Action/Adventure*: **Guardians of the Galaxy**, **Edge of Tomorrow**, **The Matrix**.  \\n   - *Philosophical/Thought‑Provoking*: **2001**, **Blade Runner**, **Arrival**, **Ex Machina**.  \\n   - *Horror/Thriller*: **The Thing**, **Annihilation**, **M3GAN**, **Coherence**.  \\n\\n2. **Time Commitment:**  \\n   - *Under 2\\u202fh*: Most of the classic titles, **Moon**, **Coherence**, **Primer**.  \\n   - *Epic Marathon*: **Dune**, **Interstellar**, **The Lord of the Rings**‑style (though not sci‑fi, but similar length).  \\n\\n3. **Family‑Friendly:**  \\n   - **Back to the Future**, **The Martian**, **Star Wars** (any), **Guardians of the Galaxy**, **Avatar**.  \\n\\n4. **If You Want Something Fresh:**  \\n   - **The Creator**, **Everything Everywhere All at Once**, **The Wandering Earth**, **Prospect**.  \\n\\n---\\n\\n### Quick “Pick‑One” Recommendations\\n\\n| Preference | One‑Movie Recommendation |\\n|------------|--------------------------|\\n| **Mind‑bending** | *Inception* (2010) |\\n| **Space‑Odyssey** | *Interstellar* (2014) |\\n| **AI & Ethics** | *Ex Machina* (2014) |\\n| **Dystopian Future** | *Blade Runner 2049* (2017) |\\n| **Time‑Loop Fun** | *Edge of Tomorrow* (2014) |\\n| **Alien Contact** | *Arrival* (2016) |\\n| **Classic Noir‑Sci‑Fi** | *Blade Runner* (1982) |\\n| **Indie Thriller** | *Coherence* (2013) |\\n| **Epic World‑Building** | *Dune* (2021) |\\n| **Family‑Friendly Adventure** | *Star Wars: A New Hope* (1977) |\\n\\nEnjoy your viewing! If you have a particular sub‑genre, era, or mood in mind, let me know and I can narrow the list further. 🚀🍿' additional_kwargs={'reasoning_content': 'User wants \"give me Sci-Fi movies\". Likely a list of sci-fi movies. Could ask for preferences? But they just ask. Provide a list, maybe categories, classic, recent, etc. Should be helpful. Provide brief descriptions and maybe year. Also could ask if they want recommendations. Provide varied list.'} response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 2498, 'prompt_tokens': 76, 'total_tokens': 2574, 'completion_time': 5.219281281, 'completion_tokens_details': {'reasoning_tokens': 65}, 'prompt_time': 0.003776927, 'prompt_tokens_details': None, 'queue_time': 0.40175028, 'total_time': 5.223058208}, 'model_name': 'openai/gpt-oss-120b', 'system_fingerprint': 'fp_e5b4e54fbb', 'service_tier': 'on_demand', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_provider': 'groq'} id='lc_run--01a0a9d8-c51c-7d60-bf3d-70420d7e5b6e-0' tool_calls=[] invalid_tool_calls=[] usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 76, 'output_tokens': 2498, 'total_tokens': 2574, 'output_token_details': {'reasoning': 65}}\n"
